---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageMeta from '@/components/PageMeta.astro';
import Diagram from '@/components/Diagram.astro';
import {
  STANDARDS_PROFILE,
  STANDARDS_PROFILE_VERSION,
  validateStandardsProfile,
} from '@/lib/spdtf-standards-profile.mjs';
validateStandardsProfile();
const implemented = STANDARDS_PROFILE.filter(({ implementationStatus }) => implementationStatus.startsWith('used'));
---

<Layout title="Standards profile" description="What the Property Pack candidate actually uses, what SPDTF has accepted as a bounded target, and which standards or methods remain candidates or deferred.">
  <PageMeta category="How we model SPDTF 2.0" variant="brand" updated={`Standards profile ${STANDARDS_PROFILE_VERSION} · checked 20 August 2026`} />
  <h1>Standards, vocabularies and modelling frameworks</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    This development profile separates three independent facts: the maturity of an
    external specification, the status of an OPDA decision, and whether a candidate
    actually emits or tests the technology. “Listed” never means “adopted.”
  </p>

  <h2 id="implemented">What is implemented now</h2>
  <p>
    Property Pack 0.1 is the only current SPDTF 2.0 implementation example. It uses a
    deliberately small surface: RDF 1.2 Basic-compatible Turtle, RDFS and bounded OWL
    vocabulary, XML Schema datatypes, SKOS, a tested SHACL 1.2 Core subset, four portable
    SPARQL ASK queries and limited Dublin Core Terms. It remains machine-proposed.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Technologies materially used by Property Pack 0.1</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Technology</th><th scope="col">Implementation fact</th><th scope="col">Important non-claim</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {implemented.map((record) => <tr>
        <th scope="row"><a href={record.source}>{record.name}</a></th>
        <td>{record.implementationEvidence}</td>
        <td>{record.conformance}</td>
      </tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    PROV-O has a declared prefix but no term use; that is not adoption. There is no
    current Property Pack use of DCAT, DQV, OWL-Time, DPV, ODRL, SSSOM, FIBO,
    GeoSPARQL, Schema.org, Verifiable Credentials, DID, ORG, gUFO or DASH. Cross-context
    mappings remain empty while they await working-group evidence.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stack">How the implemented stack fits together</h2>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="The current candidate stack separates graph structure and meaning, governed concepts, use-specific constraints, competency queries and metadata. Consumer projections sit downstream and do not become semantic authorities.">
flowchart TB
  accTitle: Property Pack zero point one implemented standards stack
  accDescr: RDF Basic forms the graph substrate; RDFS and OWL express meaning; SKOS manages concepts; SHACL checks declared expectations; SPARQL asks competency questions; Dublin Core supplies limited metadata; projections are downstream.
  RDF["RDF 1.2 Basic and Turtle"]:::infra
  OWL["RDFS and bounded OWL 2"]:::service
  SKOS["SKOS concept schemes"]:::data
  SHACL["SHACL 1.2 Core subset"]:::security
  SPARQL["Portable SPARQL ASK subset"]:::process
  DCT["Dublin Core Terms metadata"]:::external
  Projection["Tested consumer projections"]:::user
  RDF --> OWL
  OWL --> SKOS
  SKOS --> SHACL
  SHACL --> SPARQL
  DCT -.-> OWL
  SPARQL --> Projection
  </Diagram>

  <h2 id="maturity">Specification maturity is not OPDA adoption</h2>
  <p>
    As checked on 20 August 2026, RDF 1.2 Concepts is a W3C Candidate Recommendation
    Snapshot. RDF 1.2 Turtle, RDFS 1.2, SHACL 1.2 Core and SPARQL 1.2 Query are Working
    Drafts. OWL 2 and SKOS are W3C Recommendations. Exact candidate snapshots remain
    pinned for reproducibility until a deliberate update reruns feature probes.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Three status axes that must be read together</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Axis</th><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Examples</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Specification maturity</th><td>What does the owning standards body call this exact snapshot?</td><td>Recommendation, Candidate Recommendation, Working Draft, Note, community report.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">OPDA governance status</th><td>What has SPDTF decided about it?</td><td>Accepted bounded target, candidate, conditional, deferred, blocked or historical.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Implementation status</th><td>What terms or features does this candidate actually use and test?</td><td>Used and tested, used without a separate conformance receipt, prefix only, or not used.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="mechanisms">Reuse, reference, map and mint</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Only four relationship mechanisms are allowed in the profile</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Mechanism</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Minimum evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>reuse</code></th><td>Use exact external terms in the candidate.</td><td>Version, term list, licence/governance fit and feature tests.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>reference</code></th><td>Use a specification or method as design evidence without importing it.</td><td>Question addressed, edition and explicit non-conformance boundary.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>map</code></th><td>Keep local meaning and record a qualified relation to another term.</td><td>Mapping strength, justification, owner, confidence and review.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><code>mint</code></th><td>Create an SPDTF term when no governed reuse fits.</td><td>Namespace authority, semantic owner, evidence and non-reuse rationale.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="upper-ontology">Upper ontologies and design methods</h2>
  <p>
    UFO and OntoClean are method candidates, not adopted dependencies. gUFO is a separate
    OWL implementation and is not imported or used. DOLCE is PDTF 1.0 historical
    comparison evidence. See the <a href="/spdtf-2/ontologies/modelling-rules#upper">upper-ontology decision boundary</a>
    before interpreting any of these rows.
  </p>

  <h2 id="register">Detailed standards register</h2>
  <p>
    Every record names an exact boundary, current external maturity, actual implementation,
    OPDA governance status, mechanism, owner, evidence and re-open trigger. The source
    link is the primary specification or method source, not a search result.
  </p>
  <div class="card-grid standards-profile-grid">
    {STANDARDS_PROFILE.map((record) => <article class="card">
      <h3><a href={record.source}>{record.name}</a></h3>
      <dl>
        <dt>Current specification</dt><dd>{record.specificationMaturity} · {record.exactSnapshot}</dd>
        <dt>Candidate pin</dt><dd>{record.candidateSnapshot}</dd>
        <dt>Implementation</dt><dd>{record.implementationStatus}. {record.implementationEvidence}</dd>
        <dt>Governance</dt><dd>{record.governanceStatus}</dd>
        <dt>Purpose</dt><dd>{record.purpose}</dd>
        <dt>Feature boundary</dt><dd>{record.conformance}</dd>
        <dt>Mechanism</dt><dd><code>{record.mechanism}</code></dd>
        <dt>Owner</dt><dd>{record.owner}</dd>
        <dt>Decision evidence</dt><dd>{record.evidence}</dd>
        <dt>Re-open trigger</dt><dd>{record.reopenTrigger}</dd>
        <dt>Last checked</dt><dd>{record.lastChecked}</dd>
      </dl>
    </article>)}
  </div>

  <h2 id="projections">Projections are not profile mechanisms</h2>
  <p>
    JSON Schema, JSON-LD, forms, APIs, website pages, PDF and Markdown are generated
    projections. Their package version and validation belong in the
    <a href="/spdtf-2/outputs">outputs register</a>; “projection” is not a fifth standards
    relationship mechanism.
  </p>
</Layout>
